  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 433 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging is centered to our east with a weak low and \nstationary frontal boundary located to our north. Some shower \nactivity has continued to our north with low-end rain chances in \nsouthwest Virginia. Otherwise, record high temperatures are expected \nagain today with the records and year of occurrence listed below: \n\nRecord highs Today: \nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville     Tri-Cities      Oak Ridge \n                 \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)  \n\nSimilarly warm temperatures are expected tonight with the system to \nthe north deepening as it moves into the eastern Great Lakes by \nmorning. The frontal boundary associated with the system will \napproach from the west through the day ahead of a Canadian high. \nMoisture will be sufficient for low-end rain chances but limited \nbased on moisture and fast timing. The front will move through the \narea by Friday night, bringing northwesterly flow and much cooler \ntemperatures to the region by Saturday. With lingering low-level \nmoisture, PoPs will persist over the mountains and foothills into \nthe daytime. With 850mb temperatures reaching near to below 0 \nCelsius some mix with snow is possible across the highest peaks. \nOtherwise, a breezy day with highs in the 50s is expected for most \nof the region. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday morning, the high will be centered just \nto our north and east with subsidence, leading to strong radiational \ncooling conditions. The first frost of the year is very likely for \nPlateau, northeast Tennessee, and the Tennessee/North Carolina \nmountains at a minimum. By Sunday, height rises are expected as \ntroughing moves off to the east, allowing temperatures to rise back \ninto the 60s. Another cool morning is expected on Monday with \ncontinued subsidence. High pressure will remain in place for the \nmiddle of the week with gradual height rises promoting a continued \nwarming trend.",
